{
  "_doc": "ROUND 16 — INSTANCES: twenty places on the overworld that are a DOOR, not a dot. \"Add about 20 new overworld events that feature an instance system. It could be an abandoned building, a cave entrance, a dungeon entrance. These take you to a different zone similar to our current dungeon system where a quest can be found, or a boss to kill. Have one be a dragons lair, which can both be discovered randomly or through a quest from someone in town or elsewhere.\" Each entry is a MOUTH (a set piece on the surface, `plan`) plus an INTERIOR (`interior`, handed to createDungeon as its `shape`) plus what is down there (`holds`) and what clearing it pays (`gives`). FIELDS. `id` is the key everything else refers to. `name` is what the mouth is called when the map slot has no name of its own. `icon` is the map glyph and is unique across this file AND data/landmarks.json, so a cave and a cistern are not the same dot. `blurb` is what you see from outside, before you go in. `weight` is the relative chance of this one winning a slot it fits. `on` is matched against a slot's tag set the same way data/landmarks.json's `on` is — the slot kind (landmark, pass, dungeon, crossing, road, junction) and the ground tags (grass, farmland, scrub, forest, wetland, river, coast, highland, mountain). `biomes` is the ground it will stand on, or [\"any\"]. `minBand`/`maxBand` are zone bands (0 settled … 7 unforgiving), inclusive. `townGap` is metres of clear ground it needs between itself and the nearest settlement's watch. `arch` picks the archway look from GATE_ARCHES in js/dungeon.js (stone / rough / timber / pale) — a cave with a dressed lintel over it reads as a door somebody made, which is what a cave is not. `interior` is the createDungeon `shape` override plus three fields createDungeon does not read: `overLevel` (how far above the zone's mid level the inside is), `packs` (bodies per room, replacing balance.dungeon.packsPerRoom) and `families` (which bestiary families fill it — filter the defsFor pool by these and FALL BACK to the unfiltered pool if the filter empties, or a warren quietly fills with bandits). `holds` is the point of the whole feature and is never empty: `boss` (either a forced bestiary boss `id`, or a `family` + `rank` + `modifiers` to roll one), `quest` (a job FOUND inside — `kind` is a quests.js kind, `to` is \"here\" / \"town\" / \"instance:<id>\", `found` is the object that starts it), `prisoner` (who is down there and which faction thanks you), `cache` (a chest grade from balance.chests.kinds, placed in the boss room when `room` says so) and `lore` (a thing to read). `gives` is paid ONCE, when the boss goes down, and uses data/landmarks.json's vocabulary — xp, loot (a rarity floor), standing (a data/factions.json key), perkPoint, revealZone, clears — plus `gold` as a [low, high] span, which is the one key the landmark payout switch does not already handle. `discovery` is \"random\" (you find it by walking), \"quest\" (a town job puts it on the map and it is not rolled onto slots otherwise) or \"both\". The dragon's lair is \"both\", because that is what was asked for. Original names and ideas only.",
